16.6 Marketing Budgeting Methodologies
Common Budgeting Approaches
Method
How It Works / When to Use
Percentage of Revenue
Most common method: allocate 5–15% of projected revenue to marketing. Simple but backward-looking — ties marketing investment to past performance rather than growth opportunity.
Zero-Based Budgeting
Build the budget from zero each period by justifying every line item. Eliminates waste but is time-intensive. Best for mature businesses re-evaluating spend mix.
Objective-Based
Define what you want to achieve (1,000 new MQLs, 20% revenue growth), calculate what it will cost to achieve it, that is your budget. Most strategically sound, but requires accurate performance benchmarks.
Competitive Parity
Match competitors' estimated spend. Defensive strategy; does not optimise for your specific market position or opportunity.


Cost Structure: Fixed vs. Variable
Fixed costs: Team headcount, tools and subscriptions, agency retainers — committed regardless of campaign activity
Variable costs: Media spend, freelancer costs, event costs — scale up or down with activity
Rule of thumb for allocation: Technology and people costs should not exceed 40% of total marketing budget. Media and content production should receive at least 60% to ensure the investment generates market reach.

Budget Review Cadence
Monthly: Actual vs. budget review by channel. Identify over- and under-spend.
Quarterly: Reallocate based on performance data. Move budget from underperforming channels to overperforming ones.
Annual: Full budget review and rebuild for the following year. Zero-base high-cost line items.

🔗 See Also
The 70/20/10 channel allocation framework (Section 13.7) applies directly to how marketing budget is distributed across core, emerging, and experimental channels.


16.7 Market Structure: Local, National, and International Dynamics
Marketing strategy doesn't operate in a vacuum — it operates in a specific market structure, and that structure shapes what's possible, what's profitable, and what's wise. The same strategy that generates compounding returns in a national market can be a waste of money in a local one, and dangerously naive in an international one.
Local Markets
In local markets, reputation is everything. Word travels fast. A single unhappy customer can cost dozens of relationships.
Community relationships matter more than advertising reach
Word-of-mouth is the primary channel — invest in product experience first
Speed of service and personal attention are differentiators that large competitors cannot easily replicate

National Markets
As you scale to national reach, reputation becomes abstract. Customers rely on reviews, ratings, and brand signals rather than personal knowledge.
Brand consistency across regions becomes critical — localisation in tone, not in values
Paid channels scale while word-of-mouth becomes a support channel
Customer service becomes systematic rather than personal — processes replace relationships

International Markets
Crossing borders introduces new complexity. What works in one country may fail — or offend — in another.
Platform dominance varies widely: Facebook in Bangladesh, WeChat and Douyin in China, Line in Thailand, WhatsApp for commerce in India and the Middle East
Payment preferences differ significantly: Cash on delivery dominates in many emerging markets; instant payment apps dominate in others
Cultural context changes every message: Images, colour symbolism, humour, formality norms all require adaptation
Regulatory environments diverge: Data privacy laws (GDPR in Europe, PDPA in Thailand), advertising restrictions, content regulations

💡 The Principle That Does Not Change
Regardless of scale — local, national, or international — the foundation remains the same: deliver genuine value, keep your promises, treat people fairly.
Local markets punish dishonesty immediately. National markets punish it systematically. International markets punish it catastrophically — because bad reputation now travels across borders instantly. Scale amplifies everything: your impact, your responsibility, and your risk.


✅ Key Takeaways
Strategy is not a list of tactics with a budget. It is a set of deliberate choices about where to focus — and where not to. The discipline of exclusion is as important as the discipline of commitment.
The CMO 8-week planning framework converts strategic principles into quarterly execution plans. The structure matters: customer research first, audit second, strategy third, planning last.
ABM's three tiers (1-to-1, 1-to-few, 1-to-many) enable personalisation at any scale. The common factor across all three: joint sales-marketing ownership of target accounts.
The T-shaped marketer profile — broad knowledge plus deep expertise — is more valuable for most roles than either generalist breadth or pure specialist depth.
Marketing budget allocation should follow performance data (through quarterly reallocation) and strategic intent (through objective-based annual planning). The 70/20/10 framework provides a practical allocation structure across channel maturity.
Market structure shapes what strategy is possible. Tactics that work in a national market may be irrelevant or counterproductive in a local or international one. Always strategy through the filter of your specific market context.


