Chapter 17: Emerging Trends
📖 Chapter Overview
The marketing landscape in 2026 is shaped by four structural shifts that are not optional, temporary, or limited to specific industries: AI-powered search and content, the deprecation of third-party cookies, the dominance of short-form video, and the globalisation of social commerce. This chapter covers each of these trends plus two evolving platform categories — international marketing ecosystems and Connected TV — that are reshaping where and how brands reach audiences. Understanding these trends does not mean chasing them. It means understanding which ones will affect your specific audience, channel mix, and competitive landscape — and making deliberate choices about where to invest ahead of the curve.


17.1 AI in Marketing
Artificial intelligence is not a future marketing trend — it is the present reality. As of 2026, AI has already fundamentally changed content creation, campaign optimisation, customer segmentation, predictive analytics, and customer service. The brands that treat AI as a supplement to human creativity and strategy are outperforming those that use it as a replacement for either.
AI Marketing Applications
Application
How AI Changes It
Content creation
AI assists with drafts, repurposing, and variation. Human expertise remains essential for original insight and brand voice.
Campaign optimisation
Google's Performance Max, Meta's Advantage+ — AI handles bid optimisation, audience expansion, and creative selection in real time.
Customer segmentation
AI identifies micro-segments from behavioural data that human analysis would miss.
Predictive analytics
AI predicts churn risk, purchase probability, and LTV from behavioural patterns.
Personalisation at scale
AI enables genuinely personalised email content, product recommendations, and website experiences at scale.
Chatbots and customer service
AI handles tier-1 customer queries, routes complex issues, and provides 24/7 support coverage.


⚠️ AI Dependency Risk
Over-reliance on AI for creative strategy produces content that is competent, generic, and indistinguishable from competitors using the same tools. The brands winning with AI are using it to execute human-defined strategy faster — not to generate strategy from scratch. Original thinking, genuine customer understanding, and creative judgment remain human advantages.


17.2 Generative Engine Optimisation (GEO)
GEO is covered operationally in Section 4.9. As a trend, the context matters: AI-generated answers are not replacing a portion of search results — they are replacing the search results page itself for a growing category of queries. When someone asks ChatGPT or Google Gemini how to choose an email marketing platform, they receive a synthesised answer. They don't see a list of ten blue links. They may not visit any website at all.
This changes the economics of search fundamentally. Traditional SEO earns traffic by ranking. GEO earns citation — being the source AI systems reference in their answers. The metrics are different (citation rate, share of AI-generated mentions, brand presence in AI outputs) and the optimisation signals are different (authority, clarity, structured information, frequent citation by other credible sources).
The trajectory over the next 12–24 months: AI-assisted search will continue taking share of navigational, informational, and comparison queries. The queries least affected are local (maps-dependent), transactional (intent to buy a specific item), and deeply personal. Brands that invest now in becoming citable, authoritative sources in their category will compound an advantage that is very difficult to close later. Brands that wait will face the same problem SEO late-movers faced in 2015: the positions are already taken.
