For specific GEO tactics, platform-by-platform optimisation, and the SEO-vs-GEO comparison table, see Section 4.9.


17.3 Cookieless & Privacy-First Marketing
Third-party cookies — the technology that powered cross-site tracking, retargeting, and audience measurement for two decades — are being deprecated across browsers. Apple's Safari has blocked them since 2017. Firefox since 2019. Google Chrome, which accounts for approximately 65% of global browser usage, has been rolling out restrictions progressively since 2021.
What Changes Without Third-Party Cookies
Retargeting becomes harder: Fewer third-party data signals mean audiences for retargeting rely more on first-party data and platform-owned signals
Attribution degrades: Cross-site conversion tracking becomes less reliable — reinforcing the case for Conversions API (Section 8.3)
Lookalike audiences change: Platform lookalike models shift from third-party to first-party data foundations

Privacy-First Marketing Strategies
First-party data: Email addresses, purchase histories, survey responses collected directly from customers with consent
Zero-party data: Data customers voluntarily share (preferences, quiz responses, stated interests). Most valuable because consent is explicit.
Server-side tracking: Conversions API, server-side GTM — move tracking off the browser to reduce dependency on cookies
Contextual targeting: Placing ads based on the content of the page (not the profile of the viewer) — effective and privacy-compliant
Clean rooms: Privacy-preserving data collaboration between brands and platforms (Google Ads Data Hub, Amazon Marketing Cloud)

17.4 Short-Form Video
Short-form video (under 90 seconds, typically 15–60 seconds) is the dominant content consumption format among under-40 audiences globally. TikTok established the format; Instagram Reels and YouTube Shorts have replicated it; LinkedIn and Pinterest have added their own versions.
Platform Characteristics
TikTok: Entertainment-led, high organic reach for new accounts, strong e-commerce integration via TikTok Shop, younger demographic (though broadening)
Instagram Reels: Discovery-oriented, highest reach for established accounts, strong purchase intent, broader age range than TikTok
YouTube Shorts: Connected to long-form channel strategy, search-discoverable unlike TikTok and Instagram, monetisation-eligible

Short-Form Video Best Practices
Hook in the first 2–3 seconds: 60–70% of viewers decide whether to continue or scroll based on the opening frames
No production barrier: Authentic, low-production content consistently outperforms highly produced content on TikTok and Reels
Native audio: Use trending sounds and original audio — both platforms algorithmically boost native audio engagement
Repurpose vertically: Vertical video (9:16 ratio) is native to all short-form platforms; horizontal content performs significantly worse

17.5 Social Commerce
Social commerce is covered operationally in Section 6.6. As a trend, the trajectory matters: what began as a Southeast Asian phenomenon — TikTok Shop, Shopee Live, WeChat Mini Programs — is now the fastest-growing e-commerce format globally. TikTok Shop launched in the US in 2023 and reached $20 billion in global GMV within its first year. The mechanics are different from traditional e-commerce: discovery and purchase happen in the same session, on the same platform, without the buyer ever leaving the app.
The strategic implication is significant. The traditional funnel — awareness on social, consideration on website, purchase on e-commerce platform — is collapsing for a growing category of consumer purchases. For brands selling products with strong visual appeal or strong social proof potential (fashion, beauty, food, fitness, electronics), building a social commerce capability is not optional — it's table stakes within two to three years.
The live selling dimension deserves specific attention. Live commerce (streaming product demonstrations with real-time purchase integration) already accounts for 20%+ of total e-commerce revenue in China and is growing rapidly in Southeast Asian markets. It remains underdeveloped in Western markets, which means first-mover advantage is available for brands willing to invest now.
🔗 See Also
For platform-by-platform social commerce setup guidance, key metrics, and live selling strategy, see Section 6.6.


17.6 International Marketing Ecosystems
Global marketing strategy cannot default to a Western-platform-first framework. Platform dominance, consumer behaviour, payment infrastructure, and regulatory environments vary significantly by market. Building an international marketing presence requires market-specific research, not simply translating a domestic strategy.
Key Regional Platform Ecosystems
China
WeChat: The super-app — messaging, payments, mini-programs, social media, and e-commerce in one ecosystem. Essential for any China market strategy.
Weibo: China's closest equivalent to Twitter/X — news, entertainment, celebrity culture, brand accounts
Douyin (TikTok's Chinese version): Short-form video and live e-commerce — the dominant e-commerce discovery channel for younger consumers
Baidu: Search engine (Google is blocked). SEO in China is Baidu SEO — different signals, different content requirements
Key insight: China requires an entirely separate digital strategy. VPN access to Western platforms is limited and legally ambiguous. Start with a local partner who understands the regulatory and platform landscape.

Southeast Asia
Shopee and Lazada: The dominant e-commerce platforms. More important than having a standalone website for most consumer brands entering the region.
LINE: The primary messaging platform in Thailand, Taiwan, and Japan — equivalent to WhatsApp for communication and customer service
Grab: Southeast Asia's super-app — ride-hailing, food delivery, payments, and now financial services. A marketing channel for local businesses.

Middle East
Snapchat: Outsized market share relative to Western markets, particularly in Saudi Arabia and the UAE — do not treat it as a secondary platform in this region
WhatsApp: Primary communication and increasingly commerce platform — WhatsApp Business is a priority customer service and sales channel
Instagram and TikTok: High penetration, strong influencer culture, particularly in fashion and lifestyle categories

India
YouTube: The dominant video platform with massive reach across all demographics and languages
WhatsApp Business: The primary SMB commerce and customer service channel
Short-form video: YouTube Shorts, Instagram Reels, and (increasingly) domestic platforms competing for audience
Digital payments: UPI (Unified Payments Interface) — near-ubiquitous for digital transactions; integrate early

💡 Core International Principle
Strategy must match where your audience actually is. The assumption that 'Facebook, Google, and email' covers your digital marketing is accurate in some markets and dangerously wrong in others. Before entering any new market, map the actual platform landscape and consumer behaviour patterns in that specific country — not the region, the country.


17.7 Connected TV (CTV)
CTV is covered operationally as an advertising channel in Section 8.6. As a trend, what matters is the pace of the structural shift: US adults now spend more time watching streaming content than traditional linear television, and the demographic most prized by advertisers — 25–54 — has moved disproportionately. Cord-cutting is not slowing down; it is accelerating.
For marketers, this creates a specific opportunity. Premium TV inventory — the kind of brand-building, high-attention format that used to require television budgets — is now accessible at digital minimums, with digital-quality targeting. The brand-building capabilities that were only available to large advertisers with network TV budgets are now available to mid-size businesses.
The trajectory: as more streaming platforms launch ad-supported tiers (Netflix, Disney+, Max, Peacock), CTV inventory supply is growing while quality is improving. Expect CPMs to compress over the next two years as supply expands. Brands entering CTV now have access to first-mover inventory relationships and lower average CPMs than those who wait until the channel is fully commoditised.
🔗 See Also
For platform details, targeting capabilities, minimum budgets, and creative requirements for CTV advertising, see Section 8.6.


✅ Key Takeaways
AI in marketing is not a future trend — it is the current operating environment. The question is not whether to use AI but how to use it to enhance rather than replace human strategy, expertise, and judgment.
The deprecation of third-party cookies is not a crisis for brands that have invested in first-party data. It is a competitive disadvantage for brands that have not. Building email lists, survey data, and direct customer relationships is now a strategic priority, not a nice-to-have.
Short-form video's dominance among under-40 audiences is structural, not cyclical. If your audience includes anyone under 40, short-form video needs to be in your channel strategy.
GEO is the most significant new discipline in search marketing since mobile. As AI-generated answers displace traditional search results, visibility requires a different set of optimisation actions than traditional SEO.
International marketing requires market-specific platform strategies, not translated domestic strategies. China, Southeast Asia, the Middle East, and India each have fundamentally different digital ecosystems.
CTV advertising offers TV-quality reach with digital-quality targeting. For brands that already have video assets and meaningful awareness budgets, it provides access to premium, cord-cutting audiences that traditional digital channels miss.


