Exercise 1: Audit Your First-Party Data Strategy
Inventory every data source you currently have that does not depend on third-party cookies: email list size and quality, CRM contact count, survey response database, loyalty programme membership, direct customer feedback. Estimate what percentage of your current marketing activity could survive the complete loss of third-party tracking data. If the answer is less than 50%, build a 6-month plan to shift that ratio toward first-party dependency.
Exercise 2: Short-Form Video Experiment
Create one piece of short-form video content (under 60 seconds) on the platform most relevant to your audience. Do not use professional production — use a phone. The purpose is to learn: how does the platform's algorithm respond to your content? How does your audience respond? What content style feels most natural and generates the most engagement? Data from a real experiment beats theory from a strategy document.


Appendix A: Glossary of Key Terms
50+ essential digital marketing terms, defined in plain language.

Term
Definition
A/B Testing
A controlled experiment comparing two versions of a marketing element (headline, CTA, layout) to determine which performs better. Requires pre-defined metrics, sufficient sample size, and statistical significance before declaring a winner.
AARRR (Pirate Metrics)
A growth framework covering Acquisition, Activation, Retention, Revenue, and Referral — the five stages of the customer lifecycle. Developed by Dave McClure.
ABM (Account-Based Marketing)
A B2B strategy that identifies specific high-value accounts and builds personalised marketing campaigns for those accounts, rather than casting wide and filtering down.
Anchor Effect
A cognitive bias where the first price or number a person sees influences their perception of subsequent prices. Used in pricing strategy (showing higher 'original' price alongside a discounted price).
Attribution Model
A framework for assigning credit for a conversion to the marketing touchpoints that influenced it. Common models: Last Click, First Click, Linear, Time Decay, and Data-Driven.
Behavioural Segmentation
Dividing an audience based on their actions and behaviours rather than demographics — what they clicked, what they bought, how often they visit, and what content they consume.
Buyer Persona
A semi-fictional representation of an ideal customer segment, built from research. Includes situation, goals, obstacles, decision criteria, and language patterns.
Buying Committee
The group of stakeholders involved in a B2B purchase decision. Typically 5–10 people including technical evaluators, financial decision-makers, end users, and executive sponsors.
CAC (Customer Acquisition Cost)
Total marketing and sales spend divided by the number of new customers acquired in a given period. A core unit economics metric.
CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift)
A Core Web Vital measuring visual stability — how much page content shifts unexpectedly as it loads. Target: < 0.1.
Cookieless Tracking
Measurement methods that do not rely on third-party cookies — including first-party data, server-side tracking (Conversions API), and contextual targeting.
CPC (Cost Per Click)
The average amount paid for each click on an advertisement. A standard paid media metric.
CPM (Cost Per Mille)
The cost per 1,000 ad impressions. A standard measurement for display, video, and programmatic advertising.
CRO (Conversion Rate Optimisation)
The systematic practice of testing and improving website elements to increase the percentage of visitors who complete a desired action.
CTR (Click-Through Rate)
The percentage of people who click a link or ad after seeing it. Formula: Clicks ÷ Impressions × 100.
CTOR (Click-to-Open Rate)
The percentage of people who clicked a link within an email, among those who opened it. Measures email content quality independent of subject line performance.
CTV (Connected TV)
Television content delivered via internet-connected devices — Smart TVs, streaming sticks (Roku, Fire TV), and gaming consoles. A growing advertising channel.
Customer Journey
The complete sequence of interactions a customer has with a brand — from first awareness through purchase and post-purchase, including all touchpoints across all channels.
Data-Driven Attribution
An attribution model that uses machine learning to assign conversion credit based on the actual paths users take, weighting touchpoints by their statistical contribution to conversion.
Drip Campaign
A sequence of pre-written emails sent on a schedule or triggered by user actions, designed to nurture leads over time.
E-E-A-T
Google's quality framework: Experience, Expertise, Authoritativeness, Trustworthiness. Used by human quality raters and increasingly influences algorithmic ranking, especially for health, finance, and legal content.
Engagement Rate
A social media metric measuring the ratio of interactions (likes, comments, shares, saves) to reach or followers. Formula varies by platform and use case.
First-Party Data
Data collected directly from your own customers and audience — email addresses, purchase histories, behavioural data on your own website. Increasingly valuable as third-party cookies are deprecated.
GEO (Generative Engine Optimisation)
The practice of optimising content and brand presence to appear in AI-generated answers from ChatGPT, Google Gemini, Perplexity AI, and similar systems.
ICP (Ideal Customer Profile)
A description of the type of organisation or person that gets the most value from your product, is easiest to sell, and is most likely to stay and refer others.
INP (Interaction to Next Paint)
A Core Web Vital measuring page responsiveness — how quickly a page responds to user input. Target: < 200ms.
Jobs-to-Be-Done (JTBD)
A framework for understanding customer motivation: customers do not buy products, they 'hire' them to make progress from a current frustrating situation to a desired outcome.
LCP (Largest Contentful Paint)
A Core Web Vital measuring loading performance — how long until the main content of a page is visible to the user. Target: < 2.5 seconds.
Lead Scoring
A system that assigns numerical values to leads based on demographic fit and behavioural engagement, to prioritise follow-up by sales or marketing automation.
Loss Aversion
A cognitive bias (documented by Kahneman & Tversky) where potential losses feel approximately twice as painful as equivalent gains feel good. Used in CRO and copywriting to frame offers.
Lookalike Audience
A targeting capability offered by advertising platforms that identifies new users who statistically resemble your existing customers or website visitors.
LTV (Lifetime Value / CLV)
The total revenue a customer generates over the course of their relationship with the brand. Formula: Average purchase value × Purchase frequency × Customer lifespan.
Marketing Automation
Software that automatically performs marketing tasks triggered by user behaviour or time-based rules — email sequences, lead scoring, CRM updates, and personalised content delivery.
MQL (Marketing Qualified Lead)
A lead that has shown sufficient intent and fit to warrant sales contact, as defined by pre-agreed criteria between marketing and sales.
North Star Metric
The single metric that best captures the core value a product delivers to customers. Connects business growth to genuine customer value.
Omnichannel
A marketing approach that provides a seamless, integrated experience across all channels and touchpoints — so the customer experiences the brand consistently regardless of how they interact.
Owned Media
Marketing channels a brand controls directly: website, email list, social profiles, app. As distinct from paid media (advertising) and earned media (press, reviews, word-of-mouth).
Paid Media
Advertising channels where a brand pays for placement: search ads, social ads, display, programmatic, and sponsored content.
PLG (Product-Led Growth)
A business strategy where the product itself drives acquisition, retention, and expansion — through free tiers, viral mechanics, and self-service onboarding.
PQL (Product Qualified Lead)
A lead who has experienced genuine value in the product (via free trial or freemium tier) and is therefore likely to convert to a paid customer.
ROAS (Return on Ad Spend)
Revenue generated per unit of advertising spend. Formula: Revenue from ads ÷ Ad spend. A standard paid media efficiency metric.
Retargeting
Serving ads to users who have previously interacted with your brand (visited your website, viewed a product, abandoned a cart) to bring them back into the funnel.
SLA (Service Level Agreement)
A formal agreement between teams (e.g., marketing and sales) defining what each commits to deliver — MQL criteria, response time, outcome reporting.
Social Proof
A psychological principle where people look to others' behaviour when uncertain. Applied in marketing through testimonials, reviews, user counts, and endorsements.
SQL (Sales Qualified Lead)
A lead that sales has validated as a genuine prospect with confirmed budget, authority, need, and timeline (BANT criteria).
T-Shaped Marketer
A professional with broad knowledge across all marketing disciplines (the horizontal bar) and deep expertise in one or two specific areas (the vertical bar).
Topic Cluster
A content architecture consisting of one 'pillar' page (comprehensive overview) supported by multiple 'cluster' pages (specific subtopics), all interlinked to build topical authority.
UTM Parameters
Tags added to URLs that allow analytics platforms to identify the source, medium, campaign, content, and keyword associated with each website visit.
Zero-Party Data
Data that customers voluntarily and explicitly share with a brand — through quizzes, preference centres, surveys, and direct communications. The most trusted form of first-party data.



